      "role": "user",
      "content": "yo i keep getting an 'Access Denied' error when my lambda trying to write logs to s3 even though i think the policy is right. can u look at this?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A \"Access Denied\" error, even with a seemingly correct policy, is often a case of missing permissions or scope issues. You're likely missing the permission to write *to* the bucket itself, not just objects within it, or perhaps the IAM role attached to your Lambda is missing the necessary trust relationship. It's a common pitfall, so try to check if the bucket policy or the IAM role policy has an explicit `s3:PutObject` for the *specific* object path, not just the bucket ARN.\n\nThis policy snippet looks okay for basic object writing, but remember that S3 policies can be tricky, especially if bucket policies are also in play. It's a good idea to check the full IAM policy attached to the Lambda execution role.\n\nIf the issue persists, check for permissions like `s3:ListBucket` if the Lambda needs to list contents before writing, though `PutObject` shouldn't strictly require it. Sometimes, the permissions need to be scoped to `arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*` to allow object creation within the bucket.\n\nI've seen this before, and it's almost always a subtle detail, like",
      "source": "target",
