    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait so you think the resource part is wrong? i see what u mean about the /* at the end. let me try updating it to \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\" and see if that works. \n\nbut what was that last bit you were saying? \"it's almost always a subtle detail, like...\" what else could it be besides the ARN syntax?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, changing the resource to include the wildcard (`\"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"`) is a very common fix for this exact problem, as it explicitly grants permission to *put objects inside* the bucket. The subtle detail I was hinting at could be a few things, like a misconfigured bucket policy overriding your IAM role, or perhaps the Lambda's execution role isn't actually attached to the Lambda function in the first place. It's easy to forget that the role needs to be correctly assigned, which is a common oversight when deploying functions.\n\nAnother possibility is that the service principal making the call (your Lambda's role) doesn't have the necessary permissions granted by the bucket's own policy. If the bucket policy is set to be private, it might be rejecting the request even if your IAM role is wide open. It's a multi-layered security system, and sometimes the gatekeeper is the bucket itself, not the key you hold.\n\nI've seen cases where the logging mechanism itself might be configured to use a different service role than the one you are editing. Just make sure the role you are modifying is the *exact* one the Lambda",
      "source": "target",
